<h2>Where the time was going</h2>

<p>
  Auditing a site is mostly pattern recognition, and a large share of it is
  mechanical: checking whether canonicals are self-referencing, whether heading
  hierarchies are sane, whether schema is present and valid, whether images have
  dimensions, whether a template change silently broke something across a
  hundred pages.
</p>

<p>
  None of that is strategy. But at portfolio scale it consumes the hours that
  should be going into strategy. The team was spending its most expensive
  resource &mdash; experienced judgment &mdash; on work that did not require
  judgment.
</p>

<h2>What I built</h2>

<h3>JavaScript bookmarklets for in-page auditing</h3>
<p>
  Small, dependency-free tools that run against the page you're already looking
  at and surface the technical attributes that matter, instantly. No tool
  switching, no export, no waiting on a crawl. The value is not that any single
  check is hard; it's that collapsing twenty manual checks into one click
  changes how often people actually run them.
</p>

<h3>REST API scripts for bulk extraction</h3>
<p>
  WordPress exposes a REST API, and so do most of the platforms in the stack.
  Scripted extraction made it possible to inventory content, metadata, and
  structural attributes across many sites at once &mdash; which is what turns a
  per-site audit into a portfolio-level analysis.
</p>

<h3>AI-assisted audit and gap analysis workflows</h3>
<p>
  I use AI where it is genuinely good: summarizing large volumes of competitive
  content, clustering topics, drafting the first pass of a gap analysis, and
  accelerating content migration. I do not use it to generate recommendations
  that go to a client unreviewed. The workflow is AI for throughput, human for
  judgment and accountability.
</p>

<h2>The result</h2>

<p>
  Manual technical analysis time dropped by an estimated 30&ndash;40% across the
  web team. The more important second-order effect: checks that used to be
  skipped under deadline pressure now happen by default, because the cost of
  running them fell to near zero.
</p>

<h2>Why I think this matters for an agency role</h2>

<p>
  Agency SEO is constrained by hours. Any strategist can identify more work than
  the retainer supports; the differentiator is being able to expand what fits
  inside it. Tooling is how I create room for strategic work without asking for
  more budget &mdash; and because I build it myself, I can shape it around how
  the team actually works rather than how a vendor assumed they would.
</p>
